 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "LSS-15 Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "The LSS-15 scores are presented as a total raw score (between 0 and 60), and a score for each subscale (between 0 and 12). In addition, scores are presented as percentile ranges comparing the respondents score against an athlete normative dataset (Teques et al., 2020). A percentile score of 50 represents average scores compared to the comparison group of athletes.\n\nHigher scores are indicative of a more positive relationship between athlete and coach, except for on the Autocratic Behaviour subscale where lower scores are indicative of a better relationship. Democratic behaviour and Autocratic Behaviour are negatively correlated, so if scores on both subscales are high (above 80th percentile) or low (below 20th percentile) then it may indicate the athlete has not responded accurately. One would normally expect that when Autocratic Behaviour is low Democratic Behaviour will be high, and vice versa.\n\nThe five subscales are:\n1. Training and instruction (Items 1, 2, 3): Examines coaches’ focus on training, skill instruction and sport strategies to improve the performance levels of athletes.\n2. Democratic behaviour (Items 4, 5, 6): The degree to which coaches share sport-related decision-making with their athletes, such as practice methods, game tactics and strategies.\n3. Autocratic behaviour (Items 7, 8, 9): The degree to which coaches tend to maintain control of all decision and coaching behaviour which involves independent decision making and stresses personal authority.\n4. Social support (Items 10, 11, 12): Measures coaches’ actions to create a supportive team atmosphere and coaching behaviour which is characterised by a concern for the welfare of individual athletes, a positive group atmosphere, and warm interpersonal relations with members.\n5. Positive feedback (Items 13, 14, 15): Examines coaches’ abilities to provide positive and encouraging performance feedback and coaching behaviour which reinforces an athlete by recognising and rewarding good performance.\n\nUpon first administration a plot is displayed showing the total score and the subscale score percentiles with shading in the background to aid interpretation. Note the inverse colouring for the Autocratic Behaviour subscale where lower scoring is more indicative of a better coach-athlete relationship. Upon multiple administration two plots are presented: (i) a subscale raw score plot to help indicate if change has occurred and (ii) a total score and subscale percentile plot.",
    "source_url": null,
    "origin": "retained_context_section",
    "context_id": "C6"
   }
  ],
